## 4. Core Rules

### 4.1 Setup
- 4 players per match (prototype fixed count).
- Standard 52-card deck, single deck, reshuffled each round.
- Each player starts with 0 items in inventory (capacity 3).

### 4.2 Round Flow
1. **Deal:** Two cards to each player, two to the dealer (one dealer card face-down/hidden from players).
2. **Player turns (sequential, visible to all):** each player chooses one of:
   - **Hit** — draw a card.
   - **Stand** — lock in current hand.
   - **Use item** — spend one inventory item (does not end the turn unless the item implies it — see §4.5).
   A player may use an item and then still hit/stand in the same turn, EXCEPT items that explicitly consume the full turn.
3. **Dealer resolves:** reveal hidden card, dealer draws per standard blackjack dealer rules (hit until 17+).
4. **Scoring & ranking:** all players' hands are scored (standard blackjack values, bust = lowest possible rank). Rank all 4 players highest to lowest.
   - **Tie-breaker:** fewer cards in hand wins the tie.
5. **Rewards & risk:**
   - Top finisher receives **one random item** (if inventory not full; if full, item is forfeit — do not auto-discard another item).
   - Lowest finisher faces **Roulette Check** (§4.3).
6. **Next round** begins immediately unless only one player remains (see §4.4).

### 4.3 Roulette Check
- A 6-chamber revolver, **one chamber loaded**, re-randomized fresh every check (i.e., not a persistent shared cylinder across rounds — each check is an independent 1-in-6 unless an item modifies it).
- Resolve: loaded → player is **eliminated** (permanent for the match). Empty → player continues to next round.
- This is the core tension beat — treat it as a first-class animated/dramatized moment in the UI, not a silent dice roll.

### 4.4 End Game
- Elimination is **permanent** for the match — no respawns.
- Match ends when **one player remains** — that player is the winner.
- Show a **Victory Screen** with a restart option that resets to a fresh 4-player lobby.

### 4.5 Items (5 prototype items, hold up to 3)

| Item | Effect | Turn cost |
|---|---|---|
| **Peek** | Preview the result of the next roulette check (yours or a targeted player's — pick one at implementation time, default: your own next check) | Does not consume the turn |
| **Force Hit** | Force a target player to draw a card on their next turn | Does not consume the turn |
| **Card Swap** | Trade one of your cards with one card of a target player | Does not consume the turn |
| **Shield** | Block the result of one roulette check entirely (consumed automatically when it would trigger, no manual activation needed at resolution time) | N/A (passive once held, consumed on use) |
| **Redraw** | Discard one of your cards and draw a replacement | Does not consume the turn |

Design intent: no item should feel mandatory every turn — the choice to **save vs. burn** an item is itself the core decision the pillars call for. Do not let any item bypass the hit/stand decision entirely; items augment, they don't replace, the blackjack decision.

