## 6. Technical Stack (recommended)

**Do not use Unreal or Godot for this build** — the game is a synced state machine rendered as UI, not a rendering-heavy real-time simulation. A web-first stack ships the fastest playable prototype and is trivially wrapped into a native app later.

| Layer | Choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Frontend framework | **React + TypeScript**, bundled with **Vite** | fast dev loop, standard for Cursor-assisted builds |
| Styling | **Tailwind CSS** | fast iteration on a themed UI, easy to keep the color system consistent |
| Client state | **Zustand** | lightweight, avoids Redux boilerplate for a state-machine-shaped app |
| Realtime multiplayer | **Colyseus** (Node.js authoritative server) | purpose-built for room-based turn games, handles state sync, is a natural fit for the `MatchState` shape above |
| Transport | WebSockets (via Colyseus) | needed for "constant player interaction" pillar — all players see turns live |
| Optional 3D/animation accents | **React Three Fiber** + `@react-three/drei`, or `@react-three/uikit` if you want the UI itself rendered in the 3D canvas | use ONLY for the roulette chamber spin and card flip/deal animations — see §8 |
| Deployment | Vercel/Netlify (client) + Fly.io/Render (Colyseus server) | zero-friction to get a shareable link to playtesters |
| Later: native app | **Capacitor** wrapping the same React build | do this only after the core loop is validated |

### 6.1 Why authoritative server, not peer-to-peer
Roulette outcomes and card draws must be resolved server-side and revealed to clients — never trust the client to generate its own random outcome, or item effects like "Peek" become exploitable. Colyseus's room simulation loop is the natural home for round resolution logic (§4.2–4.3).

