## 7. UI / Visual Direction

### 7.1 Mood
Noir casino tension — closer to a dim back-room card table than a bright mobile-casual game. The palette should make the **roulette moment read as genuinely dangerous** against an otherwise calm blackjack table, so the danger color must be reserved and not used decoratively elsewhere.

### 7.2 Color system (color-theory rationale)

Base: a **near-black, slightly warm charcoal** background (not pure black — pure black flattens depth and reads cheap). Felt-green accent for the "table" identity. A single reserved red for danger/roulette so it never gets diluted.

| Token | Hex | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `bg.base` | `#14120F` | app background, warm near-black |
| `bg.surface` | `#1E1B17` | card/panel surfaces, table felt backing |
| `felt.primary` | `#0F3D2E` | table felt green (blackjack identity) |
| `felt.line` | `#1C5B45` | felt highlights/borders |
| `accent.gold` | `#C9A24B` | items, currency-feeling UI, "you're winning" affordance |
| `danger.red` | `#B3261E` | **reserved exclusively for roulette/elimination states** — never used for generic errors or unrelated UI |
| `danger.glow` | `#E5484D` | roulette check animation glow, pulsing during the check |
| `text.primary` | `#EDE6D6` | main text, warm off-white (not pure white, keeps the noir mood) |
| `text.muted` | `#A69C89` | secondary text, turn hints |
| `state.safe` | `#4E9E6B` | "survived roulette" / positive round outcome |
| `state.eliminated` | `#5C5650` | desaturated, used to grey out an eliminated player's seat |

Rationale: gold and green are analogous-adjacent (both read as "table/wealth"), so they coexist without competing. Red is the sole complementary jolt in the palette — reserving it strictly for danger keeps its impact from wearing out, which matters directly for the "high tension" pillar.

### 7.3 Typography
- Headings / round announcements: a slab or display serif (e.g. `"Fraunces"` or `"Bricolage Grotesque"`) — gives a "dealer's placard" feel.
- UI/body text: a clean grotesk (e.g. `"Inter"` or `"IBM Plex Sans"`) for legibility at small sizes on player seats.

### 7.4 Layout
- Central table view, 4 player seats arranged around an oval felt table, dealer hand at the top-center.
- Each seat shows: name, hand (face-up cards), running score, item slots (max 3, shown as small icon slots), and a status ring (active turn = gold ring, eliminated = greyed out).
- Turn order and current phase (`playerTurns`, `dealerResolve`, `rouletteCheck`, etc.) shown as a persistent status strip so players always know what's happening — critical for the "constant interaction" pillar when it's not your turn.

### 7.5 On React Three Fiber / `@react-three/uikit`
Use these **only for two specific moments**, not for the whole UI — a fully 3D UI adds build time without helping validate the core loop:

1. **Roulette Check scene** — a small R3F canvas rendering a revolver cylinder spin/stop, camera pushed in tight, timed to a few seconds of suspense before resolving. This is the single highest-leverage place to spend animation budget, since it's the emotional peak of the loop.
2. **Card deal/flip** — simple 3D card flip transform (can also be done in pure CSS 3D transforms via Tailwind/Framer Motion if you want to skip R3F entirely for this one).

If you want the rest of the interface (buttons, panels, item bar) rendered inside the same 3D canvas for visual consistency, `@react-three/uikit` is the right tool — but for a fast prototype, it's reasonable to keep the main table as standard DOM/Tailwind and reserve the 3D canvas strictly for the roulette moment. Recommend starting DOM-first and only pulling the roulette scene into R3F; revisit whole-UI-in-3D only after the core loop is validated.

